// Policy receives the capability; bootstrap code selects the adapter.
final class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository;

    OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    void process(long orderId) {
        Order order = repository.findById(orderId);
        repository.save(order);
        Money fee = new Money(100); // ordinary value construction
    }
}

OrderService service = new OrderService(new MySqlOrderRepository());
```

The first design is problematic when the repository is a volatile boundary the policy should not choose. The last line still uses `new`, but it does so at the assembly boundary where concrete implementations must be selected.

// High-level module defines the abstraction it needs
public interface OrderRepository {
    Order findById(Long id);
    void save(Order order);
}

// Low-level module depends on the abstraction (implements it)
@Repository
public class MySQLOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    public Order findById(Long id) {
        // JDBC or JPA code to query MySQL
        return new Order(id, "Sample Item");
    }
    public void save(Order order) { /* persist to MySQL */ }
}

// High-level business logic depends on abstraction, not concrete class
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repository; // interface, not MySQLOrderRepository

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository; // Spring injects concrete implementation
    }

    public Order processOrder(Long orderId) {
        var order = repository.findById(orderId); // works with any implementation
        // business logic here
        repository.save(order);
        return order;
    }
}

```java
// Application policy declares the capability it needs.
interface PaymentGateway {
    void charge(Money amount);
}

final class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;

    // DI: the collaborator is supplied from outside.
    OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    void checkout(Money total) {
        gateway.charge(total);
    }
}

final class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public void charge(Money amount) { /* call Stripe SDK */ }
}

// Manual assembly; Spring could automate this construction.
OrderService service = new OrderService(new StripeGateway());
```

DI describes how `gateway` reaches `OrderService`. DIP describes why the service depends on the application `PaymentGateway` contract instead of the Stripe detail.

```java
interface PaymentGateway {
    boolean charge(int cents);
}

final class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;

    OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) { this.gateway = gateway; }

    String checkout(int cents) {
        return gateway.charge(cents) ? "confirmed" : "declined";
    }
}

final class FakeGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    boolean approved;
    int charged;

    FakeGateway(boolean approved) { this.approved = approved; }
    public boolean charge(int cents) { charged++; return approved; }
}

FakeGateway fake = new FakeGateway(false);
OrderService service = new OrderService(fake);
assertEquals("declined", service.checkout(500));
```

The result assertion proves the policy branch. Checking `fake.charged` is useful only if call count is part of the required behavior.
